{"id":"references/Petersen/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch01_RiemannianMetrics#def:pet-ch1-riemannian-manifold","title":"def:pet-ch1-riemannian-manifold","path":"references/Petersen/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch01_RiemannianMetrics.md","line":10,"body":"","kind":"definition","status":"not_started","tex":"references/Petersen/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch01_RiemannianMetrics.tex#def:pet-ch1-riemannian-manifold","uses":[],"inspired_by":[],"bindings":[],"is_index":false,"body_html":"","formal":[],"tex_content":{"path":"references/Petersen/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch01_RiemannianMetrics.tex","label":"def:pet-ch1-riemannian-manifold","line":12,"source":"\\begin{definition}[Riemannian manifold]\n  \\label{def:pet-ch1-riemannian-manifold}\n\n  \\source{petersen:page-0021}\n  A \\emph{Riemannian metric} on a manifold $M$ is a family of inner products\n  $g_p$ on $T_pM$ such that $p\\mapsto g_p(X_p,Y_p)$ is smooth for all smooth\n  vector fields $X,Y$. The pair $(M,g)$ is a \\emph{Riemannian manifold}. We\n  write $g(X,Y)$ pointwise and use $g_M$ when the underlying manifold must be\n  explicit. If $\\dim M=n$, every $(T_pM,g_p)$ is linearly isometric to\n  Euclidean $n$-space.\n\\end{definition}\n\nThe elementary models below fix the metric and map conventions used throughout.","html":" <p></p><section class=\"theorem\"><div class=\"theorem-label\">Definition</div></section>[Riemannian manifold] <span class=\"tex-label\" id=\"tex-label-6465663a7065742d6368312d7269656d616e6e69616e2d6d616e69666f6c64\"></span> <p></p><span class=\"tex-source\" style=\"display:block\">Source: <span class=\"reference\">petersen:page-0021</span></span> A <em>Riemannian metric</em> on a manifold <span class=\"math\" data-display=\"false\" data-tex=\"$M$\">$M$</span> is a family of inner products <span class=\"math\" data-display=\"false\" data-tex=\"$g_p$\">$g_p$</span> on <span class=\"math\" data-display=\"false\" data-tex=\"$T_pM$\">$T_pM$</span> such that <span class=\"math\" data-display=\"false\" data-tex=\"$p\\mapsto g_p(X_p,Y_p)$\">$p\\mapsto g_p(X_p,Y_p)$</span> is smooth for all smooth vector fields <span class=\"math\" data-display=\"false\" data-tex=\"$X,Y$\">$X,Y$</span>. The pair <span class=\"math\" data-display=\"false\" data-tex=\"$(M,g)$\">$(M,g)$</span> is a <em>Riemannian manifold</em>. We write <span class=\"math\" data-display=\"false\" data-tex=\"$g(X,Y)$\">$g(X,Y)$</span> pointwise and use <span class=\"math\" data-display=\"false\" data-tex=\"$g_M$\">$g_M$</span> when the underlying manifold must be explicit. If <span class=\"math\" data-display=\"false\" data-tex=\"$\\dim M=n$\">$\\dim M=n$</span>, every <span class=\"math\" data-display=\"false\" data-tex=\"$(T_pM,g_p)$\">$(T_pM,g_p)$</span> is linearly isometric to Euclidean <span class=\"math\" data-display=\"false\" data-tex=\"$n$\">$n$</span>-space.  <p></p>The elementary models below fix the metric and map conventions used throughout.","warnings":[]},"previous":"references/Petersen/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch01_RiemannianMetrics#chap:pet-riemannian-metrics","next":"references/Petersen/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch01_RiemannianMetrics#ex:pet-ch1-euclidean-space","relations":[],"backlinks":[{"kind":"uses","id":"references/Petersen/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch01_RiemannianMetrics#ex:pet-ch1-euclidean-space","title":"ex:pet-ch1-euclidean-space","label":"ex:pet-ch1-euclidean-space"},{"kind":"uses","id":"references/Petersen/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch01_RiemannianMetrics#def:pet-ch1-riemannian-isometry","title":"def:pet-ch1-riemannian-isometry","label":"def:pet-ch1-riemannian-isometry"},{"kind":"uses","id":"references/Petersen/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch01_RiemannianMetrics#ex:pet-ch1-inner-product-space","title":"ex:pet-ch1-inner-product-space","label":"ex:pet-ch1-inner-product-space"},{"kind":"uses","id":"references/Petersen/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch01_RiemannianMetrics#def:pet-ch1-pullback-metric","title":"def:pet-ch1-pullback-metric","label":"def:pet-ch1-pullback-metric"},{"kind":"uses","id":"references/Petersen/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch01_RiemannianMetrics#def:pet-ch1-riemannian-submersion","title":"def:pet-ch1-riemannian-submersion","label":"def:pet-ch1-riemannian-submersion"},{"kind":"uses","id":"references/Petersen/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch01_RiemannianMetrics#def:pet-ch1-pseudo-riemannian","title":"def:pet-ch1-pseudo-riemannian","label":"def:pet-ch1-pseudo-riemannian"},{"kind":"uses","id":"references/Petersen/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch01_RiemannianMetrics#def:pet-ch1-signed-volume","title":"def:pet-ch1-signed-volume","label":"def:pet-ch1-signed-volume"},{"kind":"uses","id":"references/Petersen/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch01_RiemannianMetrics#def:pet-ch1-coordinate-metric","title":"def:pet-ch1-coordinate-metric","label":"def:pet-ch1-coordinate-metric"},{"kind":"uses","id":"references/Petersen/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch01_RiemannianMetrics#def:pet-ch1-frame-metric","title":"def:pet-ch1-frame-metric","label":"def:pet-ch1-frame-metric"},{"kind":"uses","id":"references/Petersen/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch01_RiemannianMetrics#def:pet-ch1-tensor-type-change","title":"def:pet-ch1-tensor-type-change","label":"def:pet-ch1-tensor-type-change"},{"kind":"uses","id":"references/Petersen/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch01_RiemannianMetrics#rem:pet-ch1-ex-1","title":"rem:pet-ch1-ex-1","label":"rem:pet-ch1-ex-1"},{"kind":"uses","id":"references/Petersen/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch01_RiemannianMetrics#rem:pet-ch1-ex-16","title":"rem:pet-ch1-ex-16","label":"rem:pet-ch1-ex-16"},{"kind":"uses","id":"references/Petersen/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch01_RiemannianMetrics#prop:pet-ch1-no-flat-immersion","title":"prop:pet-ch1-no-flat-immersion","label":"prop:pet-ch1-no-flat-immersion"},{"kind":"uses","id":"references/Petersen/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch01_RiemannianMetrics#rem:pet-ch1-ex-26","title":"rem:pet-ch1-ex-26","label":"rem:pet-ch1-ex-26"}],"copy_context":{"version":1,"level":0,"node":"references/Petersen/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch01_RiemannianMetrics#def:pet-ch1-riemannian-manifold","title":"def:pet-ch1-riemannian-manifold","tex":"\\begin{definition}[Riemannian manifold]\n  \\label{def:pet-ch1-riemannian-manifold}\n\n  \\source{petersen:page-0021}\n  A \\emph{Riemannian metric} on a manifold $M$ is a family of inner products\n  $g_p$ on $T_pM$ such that $p\\mapsto g_p(X_p,Y_p)$ is smooth for all smooth\n  vector fields $X,Y$. The pair $(M,g)$ is a \\emph{Riemannian manifold}. We\n  write $g(X,Y)$ pointwise and use $g_M$ when the underlying manifold must be\n  explicit. If $\\dim M=n$, every $(T_pM,g_p)$ is linearly isometric to\n  Euclidean $n$-space.\n\\end{definition}\n\nThe elementary models below fix the metric and map conventions used throughout.","source":"tex/references/Petersen/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch01_RiemannianMetrics.tex","description":"","dependencies":[],"dependencies_omitted":0,"unresolved_dependencies":[],"unresolved_omitted":0}}